<h2 id="applications-heading">Roller Conveyor Applications</h2>
<h3>Case and Carton Transport</h3>
<p>Roller conveyors dominate case transport in <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging line applications</a>. Sealed cases with flat bottoms ride smoothly across rollers at speeds from 30 to 200 FPM. End-of-line systems typically use <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/powered/">powered roller conveyors</a> between case packers, case sealers, labelers, and palletizers. <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">Accumulation zones</a> <a href="/conveyor-types/buffer-conveyors/">buffer cases</a> before palletizing.</p>
<h3>Distribution and Fulfillment</h3>
<p>Distribution centers use <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR roller conveyors</a> for zone-controlled transport and accumulation. Totes and shipping cartons route through pick modules, merge onto trunk lines, and accumulate before shipping sorters, where <a href="/conveyor-functions/singulation/">singulation</a> spaces them for induction. MDR provides silent zero-pressure accumulation that protects product and reduces energy consumption. Intelligrated (Honeywell), Dematic, and TGW integrate MDR roller conveyors into large-scale <a href="/resources/automated-conveyor-systems/">automated conveyor systems</a>.</p>
<h3>Pallet Handling</h3>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/cdlr-conveyors/">CDLR conveyors</a> handle loaded pallets between stretch wrappers, pallet jacks, and truck loading areas. Heavy-duty rollers with 3.5" diameter and 7-gauge wall thickness support pallet loads up to 4,000 lbs on standard frames and 10,000+ lbs on structural frames. <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/pallet/">Pallet conveyor systems</a> often include <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">right-angle transfers</a> and <a href="/conveyor-types/vertical-reciprocating/">vertical reciprocating conveyors</a> for multi-level facilities.</p>
<h3>Food and Beverage</h3>
<p><a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">Food and beverage facilities</a> use stainless steel roller conveyors with sealed bearings and open-frame construction for washdown cleaning. <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">Sanitary roller conveyors</a> feature tool-free roller removal, sloped frame surfaces that shed water, and FDA-compliant bearing seals. Dorner and mk North America manufacture purpose-built sanitary roller conveyor lines.</p>
<h3>Loading Docks and Shipping</h3>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/gravity/">Gravity roller conveyors</a> are the standard for loading dock applications. Workers push cases down a slight decline into trailers. Flexible/expandable gravity roller sections telescope to reach deep into trailers. These cost a fraction of powered systems and require zero electrical infrastructure.</p>
<h2 id="selection-heading">How to Select a Roller Conveyor</h2>
<p>Selecting the right roller conveyor requires matching six parameters to available equipment.</p>
<p><strong>1. Product bottom surface.</strong> Roller conveyors require a flat, rigid bottom surface. Soft bags, pouches, and irregular shapes fall between rollers and require belt conveyors instead. The product bottom must span at least 3 rollers at the chosen pitch.</p>
<p><strong>2. Product weight.</strong> Individual product weight determines roller diameter, wall thickness, bearing type, and frame gauge. Light cases (under 50 lbs) use 1.9" diameter rollers in light-gauge frames. Heavy pallets (over 500 lbs) require 3.5" rollers in structural frames with heavy-duty bearings.</p>
<p><strong>3. Speed and throughput.</strong> Calculate required speed from your throughput formula. <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/gravity/">Gravity systems</a> provide variable, uncontrolled speed. Powered systems provide consistent, adjustable speed. Match the conveyor speed to your upstream and downstream equipment rates.</p>
<p><strong>4. Accumulation requirement.</strong> If products must queue without backpressure, select <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR</a> or pneumatic <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation conveyors</a>. Continuous transport without accumulation allows simpler <a href="/conveyor-types/live-roller-conveyors/">live roller</a> or gravity configurations.</p>
<p><strong>5. Environment.</strong> Wet or <a href="/conveyor-types/washdown-conveyors/">washdown environments</a> require stainless steel rollers, sealed bearings, and corrosion-resistant frames. Standard warehouse environments use galvanized or painted carbon steel. High-temperature environments require special bearing lubricants and heat-resistant materials.</p>
<p><strong>6. Budget.</strong> <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/gravity/">Gravity roller</a> costs the least per foot. <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR</a> costs more upfront but saves on energy and maintenance over the conveyor's 15-20 year life. <a href="/conveyor-types/cdlr-conveyors/">CDLR</a> costs more due to heavier construction but handles loads that lighter conveyors cannot. See the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a> for factor-by-factor cost breakdowns.</p>
<h2 id="manufacturers-heading">Leading <a href="/resources/conveyor-manufacturers/">Roller Conveyor Manufacturers</a></h2>
<p>The roller conveyor market includes both component manufacturers (who make rollers and frames) and system integrators (who design and install complete systems). Key players include:</p>
